    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code with PKCE and Keycloak',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains OAuth 2 point 0 with Keycloak, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Against a real Keycloak the authorization code flow with P K C E is a handful of client settings, exact redirect addresses, S256 required, no implicit flow, no password grant, rotated refresh tokens and short code and token lives, and the app still has to check the state; a reused code or a reused refresh token ends the session at Keycloak, which a resource server that validates tokens locally cannot see until the token expires. [[slnc 600]] Think of it this way. [[slnc 300]] A coat check run by a hotel, not by the shop. You show the hotel your room key, never the shop, and the hotel gives the shop a numbered ticket that works once, and only for the person who holds the matching stub. If someone tries a ticket with the wrong stub, the hotel tears the ticket up. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online bank. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will be able to: [[slnc 300]] Name the parts of the authorization code flow and what each one protects. [[slnc 300]] Explain what P K C E stops and why a failed try burns the code. [[slnc 300]] List the client settings that make the flow safe.'),
